Implicit returns and expression-bodied functions
================================================

A function body's last expression is its return value
(implicit return). For `def name() = expr` (expression
function), the expression IS the body — no explicit return
needed. For block bodies, the last expression — whether
direct or after a sequence of statements — is the result
when the function's return type isn't `unit`.

    module sysl.tests.lang_features

Expression-bodied `def` function
--------------------------------

    def twice(x: int) -> int = x * 2

    #test
    test_expr_bodied() -> unit
        assert(twice(3) == 6, s"twice(3) should be 6, got ${twice(3)}")
        assert(twice(0) == 0, s"twice(0) should be 0, got ${twice(0)}")
        assert(twice(-5) == -10, s"twice(-5) should be -10, got ${twice(-5)}")

Block body where the last expression is the result
--------------------------------------------------

    triple(x: int) -> int
        val a = x + x
        val b = a + x
        b

    #test
    test_block_body_last_expr() -> unit
        assert(triple(3) == 9, s"triple(3) should be 9, got ${triple(3)}")
        assert(triple(7) == 21, s"triple(7) should be 21, got ${triple(7)}")

Implicit return matches explicit `return` semantics
---------------------------------------------------

    explicit_twice(x: int) -> int
        return x * 2

    #test
    test_implicit_matches_explicit() -> unit
        assert(twice(5) == explicit_twice(5),
            s"implicit/explicit twice should match: ${twice(5)} vs ${explicit_twice(5)}")
